IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ea-core`

-- Employment Act 1968 (Singapore), 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation
-- "version in force from 5/12/2025" -- LAYER 1, PART 10, SECTIONS 88 AND 88A (public holidays
-- and annual leave).  Part ea2.
-- Source text: inputs/EmA1968.txt (a converter rendering, not the official text).
--
-- Notes for the reader.
--   * Seam inputs (PLAN.md section 4) are taken as MAYBE inputs named `the answer of ...`:
--       S1/S2/S3  `the answer of s 35(a)` and `the answer of s 35(b)`  (MAYBE BOOLEAN)
--       S5        `the answer of s 41A(2)`                              (MAYBE BOOLEAN)
--       S6        `the answer of sch 3 gross rate of pay per day` and
--                 `the answer of sch 3 basic rate of pay per day`       (MAYBE NUMBER)
--     A NOTHING in an input is carried on and never turned into FALSE.
--   * Seam S4 is ANSWERED here: `s 88A the days of paid annual leave`.
--   * A plain BOOLEAN or NUMBER answer is a date or count comparison and carries no legal "no".

§ `Part 10 -- paid holidays (s 88)`

-- s 88(1): "Every employee is entitled to a paid holiday at his or her gross rate of pay on a public
-- holiday that falls during the time that he or she is employed, subject to the following".
-- src:3386 | 88.—(1) Every employee is entitled to a paid holiday at his or her
-- src:3387 | gross rate of pay on a public holiday that falls during the time that he
-- src:3388 | or she is employed, subject to the following:
@ref s 88(1) (src:3386-3388)
GIVEN `the public holiday` IS A DATE
      employment IS A `Dates of the employment`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88(1) the employee is entitled to a paid holiday at the gross rate of pay on the public holiday` MEANS
    IF `the public holiday` AT LEAST employment's `began on`
       AND (CONSIDER employment's `ended on`
            WHEN JUST last THEN `the public holiday` AT MOST last
            WHEN NOTHING THEN TRUE)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the entitlement is to a paid holiday "on a public holiday that falls during the time that he or she is employed"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 88(1)(a): "by agreement between the employer and the employee any other day or days may be
-- substituted for any one or more public holidays".
-- src:3390 | (a) by agreement between the employer and the employee any
-- src:3391 | other day or days may be substituted for any one or more
-- src:3392 | public holidays;
@ref s 88(1)(a) (src:3390-3392)
GIVEN `the employer and the employee agreed to substitute the day for a public holiday` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88(1)(a) the day may be substituted for the public holiday` MEANS
    IF `the employer and the employee agreed to substitute the day for a public holiday`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph allows substitution "by agreement between the employer and the employee" and says nothing of a day substituted without one
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 88(1)(b): "if any public holiday falls on a rest day, the working day next following that rest day
-- is a paid holiday".  The working day next following the rest day is given as a date.
-- src:3394 | (b) if any public holiday falls on a rest day, the working day
-- src:3395 | next following that rest day is a paid holiday;
@ref s 88(1)(b) (src:3394-3395)
GIVEN `the public holiday falls on a rest day` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the working day next following that rest day` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 88(1)(b) the day that is a paid holiday` MEANS
    IF `the public holiday falls on a rest day`
    THEN JUST `the working day next following that rest day`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph applies "if any public holiday falls on a rest day"; this type is a date and cannot say no, so the twin below does (fork F18)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- The twin that can say no ("if" counts, as ever): does the rule of paragraph (b) apply?
-- src:3394 | (b) if any public holiday falls on a rest day, the working day
-- src:3395 | next following that rest day is a paid holiday;
@ref s 88(1)(b) (src:3394-3395)
GIVEN `the public holiday falls on a rest day` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88(1)(b) the rule of paragraph (b) applies` MEANS
    IF `the public holiday falls on a rest day`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if any public holiday falls on a rest day"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 88(1)(c): "if any public holiday falls on a day when the employee is not required to work under his or
-- her contract of service, the employer may either pay the employee for that holiday at his or her
-- gross rate of pay or give the employee a day off in substitution for that holiday."
DECLARE `s 88(1)(c) What the employer does` IS ONE OF
    `s 88(1)(c) pays the employee for the holiday at the gross rate of pay`
    `s 88(1)(c) gives the employee a day off in substitution for the holiday`

-- src:3397 | (c) if any public holiday falls on a day when the employee is
-- src:3398 | not required to work under his or her contract of service,
-- src:3399 | the employer may either pay the employee for that holiday
-- src:3400 | at his or her gross rate of pay or give the employee a day off
-- src:3401 | in substitution for that holiday.
@ref s 88(1)(c) (src:3397-3401)
GIVEN `the public holiday falls on a day when the employee is not required to work under the contract of service` IS A BOOLEAN
      choice IS A `s 88(1)(c) What the employer does`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88(1)(c) the employer may do this` MEANS
    IF `the public holiday falls on a day when the employee is not required to work under the contract of service`
    THEN
        CONSIDER choice
        WHEN `s 88(1)(c) pays the employee for the holiday at the gross rate of pay` THEN JUST TRUE
        WHEN `s 88(1)(c) gives the employee a day off in substitution for the holiday` THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if any public holiday falls on a day when the employee is not required to work under his or her contract of service"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 88(2): "Despite subsection (1), an employee is not entitled to holiday pay for any public holiday
-- which falls on a day when the employee is on leave of absence without pay granted by the employer at
-- the employee's request."  The keel's "no-pay leave" is that leave.
-- src:3405 | (2) Despite subsection (1), an employee is not entitled to holiday
-- src:3406 | pay for any public holiday which falls on a day when the employee is
-- src:3407 | on leave of absence without pay granted by the employer at the
-- src:3408 | employee’s request.
@ref s 88(2) (src:3405-3408)
GIVEN `the answer of s 2(1) "no-pay leave"` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88(2) the employee is entitled to holiday pay for the public holiday` MEANS
    IF `the answer of s 2(1) "no-pay leave"` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "an employee is not entitled to holiday pay for any public holiday"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection only takes away holiday pay on a day of leave of absence without pay at the employee's request
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 88(3): the employee "who absents himself or herself from work on the working day immediately
-- preceding or immediately succeeding a public holiday or any day substituted therefor ... without the
-- prior consent of his or her employer or without reasonable excuse is not entitled to any holiday
-- pay for that holiday."  Read as it stands: lacking either the consent or the excuse is enough (fork F10).
-- src:3412 | (3) An employee who absents himself or herself from work on the
-- src:3413 | working day immediately preceding or immediately succeeding a
-- src:3414 | public holiday or any day substituted therefor under subsection (1)
-- src:3419 | without the prior consent of his or her employer or without reasonable
-- src:3420 | excuse is not entitled to any holiday pay for that holiday.
@ref s 88(3) (src:3412-3420)
GIVEN `the employee absented herself or himself from the working day immediately preceding or succeeding the public holiday or the day substituted for it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employee had the prior consent of the employer` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employee had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88(3) the employee is entitled to holiday pay for that holiday` MEANS
    IF `the employee absented herself or himself from the working day immediately preceding or succeeding the public holiday or the day substituted for it`
       AND ((NOT `the employee had the prior consent of the employer`) OR (NOT `the employee had a reasonable excuse`))
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is not entitled to any holiday pay for that holiday"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection takes away holiday pay only from an employee who absents himself or herself on the working day before or after the holiday without the prior consent of the employer or without reasonable excuse
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 88(4): "Despite subsection (1), any employee may be required by his or her employer to work on any
-- public holiday to which the employee would otherwise be entitled under that subsection and, in such
-- event, the employee must be paid an extra day's salary at the basic rate of pay for one day's work in
-- addition to the gross rate of pay for that day and to a travelling allowance, if payable ..., for one day."
-- The Commissioner's written order under s 41A(2) (seam S5) can direct that the entitlement to be paid
-- for extra work under s 88(4) does not apply.
-- src:3424 | (4) Despite subsection (1), any employee may be required by his or
-- src:3425 | her employer to work on any public holiday to which the employee
-- src:3426 | would otherwise be entitled under that subsection and, in such event,
-- src:3427 | the employee must be paid an extra day’s salary at the basic rate of
-- src:3428 | pay for one day’s work in addition to the gross rate of pay for that day
-- src:3429 | and to a travelling allowance, if payable to the employee under the
-- src:3430 | terms of his or her agreement with his or her employer, for one day.
@ref s 88(4) (src:3424-3430)
GIVEN `the employee is required by the employer to work on a public holiday to which the employee is entitled under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the answer of s 41A(2)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88(4) the employee is entitled to an extra day's salary at the basic rate of pay` MEANS
    IF NOT `the employee is required by the employer to work on a public holiday to which the employee is entitled under subsection (1)`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "and, in such event, the employee must be paid an extra day's salary"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE
        CONSIDER `the answer of s 41A(2)`
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "extra work under section 37(2) or (3), 38(4), 40(4) or 88(4) does not"
        WHEN JUST TRUE THEN JUST FALSE
        WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST TRUE
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the Commissioner has directed under section 41A(2) that the entitlement does not apply is not answered
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- s 88(4), the sum: "an extra day's salary at the basic rate of pay for one day's work in addition to the gross rate of pay for that
-- day and to a travelling allowance, if payable ... for one day."  Read as three amounts added: the extra day at the basic rate, the
-- gross rate for the day, and the travelling allowance for the day (0 if none is payable) (fork F17).
@ref s 88(4) (src:3424-3430)
GIVEN `the answer of sch 3 gross rate of pay per day` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the answer of sch 3 basic rate of pay per day` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the travelling allowance payable for one day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 88(4) the total the employee must be paid for the day` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the answer of sch 3 gross rate of pay per day`
    WHEN JUST gross THEN
        CONSIDER `the answer of sch 3 basic rate of pay per day`
        WHEN JUST basic THEN JUST (gross PLUS basic PLUS `the travelling allowance payable for one day`)
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the basic rate of pay for a day is worked out under the Third Schedule, which is not given
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the gross rate of pay for a day is worked out under the Third Schedule, which is not given
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- s 88(4A): what an employee outside Part 4 (not 35(b), not a 35(a) workman) may be given instead of a
-- day off or an extra day's salary.
DECLARE `s 88(4A) Time off in lieu` IS ONE OF
    `s 88(4A) part of a day off comprising` HAS hours IS A NUMBER
    `s 88(4A) a day off on a working day`

-- src:3434 | (4A) Despite subsections (1) and (4), where any employee (other
-- src:3435 | than an employee to whom Part 4 applies by virtue of section 35(b) or
-- src:3436 | who is a workman mentioned in section 35(a)) is required by his or
-- src:3437 | her employer to work on any public holiday to which the employee
-- src:3438 | would otherwise be entitled under subsection (1), the employee must
-- src:3439 | be paid the gross rate of pay for that day and may be given the
-- src:3440 | following, in lieu of a day off in substitution for that holiday or an
-- src:3441 | extra day’s salary at the basic rate of pay:
-- src:3443 | (a) part of a day off on a working day comprising such number
-- src:3444 | of hours as may be agreed between the employee and his or
-- src:3445 | her employer;
-- src:3447 | (b) in the case where there is no such agreement —
-- src:3449 | (i) part of a day off on a working day comprising 4 hours
-- src:3450 | if the employee worked on that holiday for a period
-- src:3451 | not exceeding 4 hours; or
-- src:3453 | (ii) a day off on a working day if the employee worked
-- src:3454 | on that holiday for a period of more than 4 hours.
@ref s 88(4A) (src:3434-3454)
GIVEN `the answer of s 35(a)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the answer of s 35(b)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the hours of part of a day off the employee and the employer agreed` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the hours the employee worked on the holiday` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE (`s 88(4A) Time off in lieu`)
`s 88(4A) the time off the employee may be given in lieu` MEANS
    BRANCH
        -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): subsection (4A) is for an employee "other than" one to whom Part 4 applies by virtue of section 35(b) or a workman mentioned in section 35(a); those employees are under subsection (4)
        IF `the answer of s 35(a)` EQUALS JUST TRUE OR `the answer of s 35(b)` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN NOTHING
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the employee is a 35(a) workman or within 35(b) is not answered
        IF `the answer of s 35(a)` EQUALS NOTHING OR `the answer of s 35(b)` EQUALS NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        IF `the hours of part of a day off the employee and the employer agreed` EQUALS NOTHING THEN
            IF `the hours the employee worked on the holiday` GREATER THAN 4
            THEN JUST `s 88(4A) a day off on a working day`
            ELSE JUST (`s 88(4A) part of a day off comprising` 4)
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `the hours of part of a day off the employee and the employer agreed`
            WHEN JUST agreed THEN JUST (`s 88(4A) part of a day off comprising` agreed)
            -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the agreed hours are not given (this branch is reached only after the no-agreement case above)
            WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- The twin that can say no: subsection (4A) opens "where any employee (other than an employee to whom
-- Part 4 applies by virtue of section 35(b) or who is a workman mentioned in section 35(a)) is required
-- by his or her employer to work on any public holiday to which the employee would otherwise be
-- entitled under subsection (1)".  Outside that "where" (including the "other than") the text's answer
-- is no (ruling (b) of 2026-11-05; the time-off type above cannot say no; fork F18).
-- src:3434 | (4A) Despite subsections (1) and (4), where any employee (other
-- src:3435 | than an employee to whom Part 4 applies by virtue of section 35(b) or
-- src:3436 | who is a workman mentioned in section 35(a)) is required by his or
-- src:3437 | her employer to work on any public holiday to which the employee
-- src:3438 | would otherwise be entitled under subsection (1), the employee must
-- src:3439 | be paid the gross rate of pay for that day and may be given the
-- src:3440 | following, in lieu of a day off in substitution for that holiday or an
-- src:3441 | extra day’s salary at the basic rate of pay:
-- src:3443 | (a) part of a day off on a working day comprising such number
-- src:3444 | of hours as may be agreed between the employee and his or
-- src:3445 | her employer;
-- src:3447 | (b) in the case where there is no such agreement —
-- src:3449 | (i) part of a day off on a working day comprising 4 hours
-- src:3450 | if the employee worked on that holiday for a period
-- src:3451 | not exceeding 4 hours; or
-- src:3453 | (ii) a day off on a working day if the employee worked
-- src:3454 | on that holiday for a period of more than 4 hours.
@ref s 88(4A) (src:3434-3454)
GIVEN `the answer of s 35(a)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the answer of s 35(b)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the employee is required by the employer to work on a public holiday to which the employee would otherwise be entitled under subsection (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88(4A) this rule applies to the employee` MEANS
    IF NOT `the employee is required by the employer to work on a public holiday to which the employee would otherwise be entitled under subsection (1)`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "where any employee (other than an employee to whom Part 4 applies by virtue of section 35(b) or who is a workman mentioned in section 35(a)) is required by his or her employer to work on any public holiday"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE
        IF `the answer of s 35(a)` EQUALS JUST TRUE OR `the answer of s 35(b)` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "other than an employee to whom Part 4 applies by virtue of section 35(b) or who is a workman mentioned in section 35(a)"
        THEN JUST FALSE
        ELSE
            IF `the answer of s 35(a)` EQUALS JUST FALSE AND `the answer of s 35(b)` EQUALS JUST FALSE
            THEN JUST TRUE
            -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): whether the employee is a 35(a) workman or within 35(b) is not answered
            ELSE NOTHING

-- s 88(4A): the employee "must be paid the gross rate of pay for that day".
@ref s 88(4A) (src:3434-3441)
GIVEN `the answer of sch 3 gross rate of pay per day` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 88(4A) the pay the employee must be paid for the day` MEANS
    `the answer of sch 3 gross rate of pay per day`

-- s 88(5): "An employee is not entitled, by reason of subsection (4), to receive double any housing
-- allowance or food allowance."
-- src:3458 | (5) An employee is not entitled, by reason of subsection (4), to
-- src:3459 | receive double any housing allowance or food allowance.
@ref s 88(5) (src:3458-3459)
GIVEN `the allowance is a housing allowance or a food allowance` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88(5) the employee is entitled by reason of subsection (4) to receive double the allowance` MEANS
    IF `the allowance is a housing allowance or a food allowance`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An employee is not entitled, by reason of subsection (4), to receive double any housing"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection speaks only of a housing allowance or a food allowance
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 88(6): an employee of the Government or a statutory body in an essential service.
-- src:3461 | (6) Subsection (4) does not apply to an employee who is employed
-- src:3462 | by the Government or a statutory body in any of the essential services
-- src:3463 | as defined under Part 3 of the Criminal Law (Temporary Provisions)
-- src:3464 | Act 1955, but —
@ref s 88(6) (src:3461-3464)
GIVEN employer IS A `Kind of employer`
      `the employee is employed in an essential service as defined under Part 3 of the Criminal Law (Temporary Provisions) Act 1955` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88(6) subsection (4) applies to the employee` MEANS
    IF (employer EQUALS `Employer who is the Government` OR employer EQUALS `Employer who is a statutory authority`)
       AND `the employee is employed in an essential service as defined under Part 3 of the Criminal Law (Temporary Provisions) Act 1955`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Subsection (4) does not apply to an employee who is employed"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection takes subsection (4) away only from an employee employed by the Government or a statutory body in an essential service
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3469 | (a) any such employee may, despite subsection (1), be
-- src:3470 | required by his or her employer to work on a public
-- src:3471 | holiday or part thereof to which the employee would
-- src:3472 | otherwise be entitled under that subsection; and
@ref s 88(6)(a) (src:3469-3472)
GIVEN `subsection (4) does not apply to the employee under section 88(6)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88(6)(a) the employee may be required to work on the public holiday` MEANS
    IF `subsection (4) does not apply to the employee under section 88(6)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the paragraph is about "any such employee"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3474 | (b) in any such case, the employee must be given a day or part
-- src:3475 | of a day off (as the case may be) in substitution for the
-- src:3476 | public holiday or part thereof.
@ref s 88(6)(b) (src:3474-3476)
GIVEN `subsection (4) does not apply to the employee under section 88(6)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the employee was required to work on the public holiday or part of it` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88(6)(b) the employee must be given a day or part of a day off in substitution` MEANS
    IF `subsection (4) does not apply to the employee under section 88(6)` AND `the employee was required to work on the public holiday or part of it`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the duty is owed "in any such case"
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 88(7): a public holiday on a half working day: "the gross or basic rate of pay payable is that of a
-- full working day".
-- src:3480 | (7) For the purposes of this section if any public holiday falls on a
-- src:3481 | half working day, the gross or basic rate of pay payable is that of a full
-- src:3482 | working day.
@ref s 88(7) (src:3480-3482)
GIVEN `the public holiday falls on a half working day` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the rate of pay of a full working day` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 88(7) the rate of pay payable for the public holiday` MEANS
    IF `the public holiday falls on a half working day`
    THEN `the rate of pay of a full working day`
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection applies "if any public holiday falls on a half working day"; this type is a rate and cannot say no, so the twin below does (fork F18)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- The twin that can say no ("if" counts): does the rule of subsection (7) apply?
-- src:3480 | (7) For the purposes of this section if any public holiday falls on a
-- src:3481 | half working day, the gross or basic rate of pay payable is that of a full
-- src:3482 | working day.
@ref s 88(7) (src:3480-3482)
GIVEN `the public holiday falls on a half working day` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88(7) the rule of subsection (7) applies` MEANS
    IF `the public holiday falls on a half working day`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if any public holiday falls on a half working day"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§ `Part 10 -- annual leave (s 88A)`

-- s 88A(1)(a): "7 days of paid annual leave, for the first 12 months of continuous service with the same employer".
-- src:3493 | (a) 7 days of paid annual leave, for the first 12 months of
-- src:3494 | continuous service with the same employer;
@ref s 88A(1)(a) (src:3493-3494)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 88A(1)(a) the days of paid annual leave for the first 12 months` MEANS 7

-- s 88A(1)(b): "an additional one day of paid annual leave, for every subsequent 12 months of continuous
-- service" -- one more day for each 12 months after the first.  The year is numbered 1, 2, 3 ...
-- src:3496 | (b) subject to paragraph (c), an additional one day of paid
-- src:3497 | annual leave, for every subsequent 12 months of
-- src:3498 | continuous service with the same employer;
@ref s 88A(1)(b) (src:3496-3498)
GIVEN `the year of continuous service, counting the first 12 months as 1` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 88A(1)(b) the additional days for the year of continuous service` MEANS
    IF `the year of continuous service, counting the first 12 months as 1` AT LEAST 2
    THEN `the year of continuous service, counting the first 12 months as 1` MINUS 1
    ELSE 0

-- s 88A(1)(c): "a maximum of 14 days of paid annual leave".
-- src:3500 | (c) a maximum of 14 days of paid annual leave.
@ref s 88A(1)(c) (src:3500)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 88A(1)(c) the most days of paid annual leave` MEANS 14

-- s 88A(1): "An employee who has served an employer for a period of not less than 3 months is ... entitled to"
-- the days of (a) to (c).  For a completed 12 months of continuous service.
-- src:3488 | 88A.—(1) An employee who has served an employer for a period
-- src:3489 | of not less than 3 months is, in addition to the rest days, holidays and
-- src:3490 | sick leave to which the employee is entitled under sections 36, 88 and
-- src:3491 | 89, respectively, entitled to the following:
@ref s 88A(1) (src:3488-3491)
GIVEN `the completed months of continuous service` IS A NUMBER
      `the year of continuous service, counting the first 12 months as 1` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 88A(1) the days of paid annual leave for a completed 12 months of continuous service` MEANS
    IF `the completed months of continuous service` AT LEAST 3
    THEN JUST (min (`s 88A(1)(c) the most days of paid annual leave`)
                   (`s 88A(1)(a) the days of paid annual leave for the first 12 months`
                    PLUS `s 88A(1)(b) the additional days for the year of continuous service` `the year of continuous service, counting the first 12 months as 1`))
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the entitlement is of an employee who has served an employer for a period of not less than 3 months
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 88A(3): the fractions of a day: less than one-half is disregarded; one-half or more is a day.
-- At exactly one half, a whole day.
-- src:3511 | (3) In calculating the proportionate annual leave under
-- src:3512 | subsection (2) —
-- src:3514 | (a) any fraction of a day that is less than one‑half of a day must
-- src:3515 | be disregarded; and
-- src:3520 | (b) where a fraction of a day is one-half or more, it must be
-- src:3521 | regarded as one day.
@ref s 88A(3) (src:3511-3521)
GIVEN `the proportionate annual leave in days, with any fraction` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 88A(3) the whole days of proportionate annual leave` MEANS
    FLOOR (`the proportionate annual leave in days, with any fraction` PLUS 0.5)

-- s 88A(2): "An employee who has served an employer for a period of not less than 3 months, but has not
-- completed 12 months of continuous service in any year, is entitled to annual leave in proportion to
-- the number of completed months of service in that year."  The proportion is of the full-year
-- entitlement for the year she is in (fork F11).  Rounded by subsection (3).
-- src:3504 | (2) An employee who has served an employer for a period of not
-- src:3505 | less than 3 months, but has not completed 12 months of continuous
-- src:3506 | service in any year, is entitled to annual leave in proportion to the
-- src:3507 | number of completed months of service in that year.
@ref s 88A(2) (src:3504-3507)
GIVEN `the completed months of service in total` IS A NUMBER
      `the completed months of service in that year` IS A NUMBER
      `the full-year entitlement for that year, in days` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 88A(2) with (3) the days of paid annual leave in proportion to the completed months` MEANS
    IF `the completed months of service in total` AT LEAST 3 AND `the completed months of service in that year` LESS THAN 12
    THEN JUST (`s 88A(3) the whole days of proportionate annual leave` ((`the full-year entitlement for that year, in days` TIMES `the completed months of service in that year`) DIVIDED BY 12))
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection is of an employee who has served an employer for not less than 3 months but has not completed 12 months of continuous service in the year
    ELSE NOTHING

-- Seam S4 (PLAN.md section 4) is answered here: the days of paid annual leave the employee is entitled to
-- under s 88A for a year of continuous service, whole or part.  `the answer of s 88A the days of paid annual leave`
-- in another part is this function's result.
-- src:3488 | 88A.—(1) An employee who has served an employer for a period
-- src:3489 | of not less than 3 months is, in addition to the rest days, holidays and
-- src:3490 | sick leave to which the employee is entitled under sections 36, 88 and
-- src:3491 | 89, respectively, entitled to the following:
-- src:3504 | (2) An employee who has served an employer for a period of not
-- src:3505 | less than 3 months, but has not completed 12 months of continuous
-- src:3506 | service in any year, is entitled to annual leave in proportion to the
-- src:3507 | number of completed months of service in that year.
-- src:3511 | (3) In calculating the proportionate annual leave under
-- src:3512 | subsection (2) —
@ref s 88A (src:3488-3512)
GIVEN `the completed months of continuous service` IS A NUMBER
      `the year of continuous service, counting the first 12 months as 1` IS A NUMBER
      `the completed months of service in that year` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 88A the days of paid annual leave` MEANS
    IF `the completed months of service in that year` AT LEAST 12
    THEN `s 88A(1) the days of paid annual leave for a completed 12 months of continuous service` `the completed months of continuous service` `the year of continuous service, counting the first 12 months as 1`
    ELSE
        CONSIDER `s 88A(1) the days of paid annual leave for a completed 12 months of continuous service` 12 `the year of continuous service, counting the first 12 months as 1`
        WHEN JUST full THEN `s 88A(2) with (3) the days of paid annual leave in proportion to the completed months` `the completed months of continuous service` `the completed months of service in that year` full
        -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the full-year entitlement is not given
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- s 88A(4): "Where an employee is granted leave of absence without pay by an employer at the employee's
-- request, the period of the leave is to be disregarded for the purpose of computing the period of
-- continuous service under this section."  A count of days.
-- src:3525 | (4) Where an employee is granted leave of absence without pay by
-- src:3526 | an employer at the employee’s request, the period of the leave is to be
-- src:3527 | disregarded for the purpose of computing the period of continuous
-- src:3528 | service under this section.
@ref s 88A(4) (src:3525-3528)
GIVEN `the days of continuous service` IS A NUMBER
      `the days of leave of absence without pay granted at the employee's request` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 88A(4) the days counted as continuous service` MEANS
    max 0 (`the days of continuous service` MINUS `the days of leave of absence without pay granted at the employee's request`)

-- s 88A(5): "An employee forfeits the employee's entitlement to annual leave if the employee absents
-- himself or herself from work without the employer's permission, or without reasonable excuse, for more
-- than 20% of the working days in the months or year (as the case may be) in which the employee's
-- entitlement to annual leave accrues."  Exactly 20% is not "more than 20%".
-- src:3532 | (5) An employee forfeits the employee’s entitlement to annual
-- src:3533 | leave if the employee absents himself or herself from work without
-- src:3534 | the employer’s permission, or without reasonable excuse, for more
-- src:3535 | than 20% of the working days in the months or year (as the case may
-- src:3536 | be) in which the employee’s entitlement to annual leave accrues.
@ref s 88A(5) (src:3532-3536)
GIVEN `the working days in the months or year in which the entitlement accrues` IS A NUMBER
      `the working days of absence without the employer's permission or without reasonable excuse` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88A(5) the employee keeps the entitlement to annual leave` MEANS
    IF `the working days of absence without the employer's permission or without reasonable excuse` TIMES 5 GREATER THAN `the working days in the months or year in which the entitlement accrues`
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An employee forfeits the employee's entitlement to annual leave if"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection never says she keeps the entitlement, so the case where its "if" fails is answered by the twin `s 88A(5) the employee forfeits the entitlement to annual leave` below
    ELSE NOTHING

-- The twin that asks the text's own question, with the text's own verb ("forfeits").  Absence of exactly
-- 20% is not "more than 20%", so the text's "if" fails and the answer is no.
-- src:3532 | (5) An employee forfeits the employee’s entitlement to annual
-- src:3533 | leave if the employee absents himself or herself from work without
-- src:3534 | the employer’s permission, or without reasonable excuse, for more
-- src:3535 | than 20% of the working days in the months or year (as the case may
-- src:3536 | be) in which the employee’s entitlement to annual leave accrues.
@ref s 88A(5) (src:3532-3536)
GIVEN `the working days in the months or year in which the entitlement accrues` IS A NUMBER
      `the working days of absence without the employer's permission or without reasonable excuse` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88A(5) the employee forfeits the entitlement to annual leave` MEANS
    IF `the working days of absence without the employer's permission or without reasonable excuse` TIMES 5 GREATER THAN `the working days in the months or year in which the entitlement accrues`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An employee forfeits the employee's entitlement to annual leave if"
    ELSE JUST FALSE

-- s 88A(6): for a workman mentioned in s 35(a) or an employee to whom Part 4 applies by virtue of s 35(b).
-- src:3540 | (6) In the case of an employee to whom Part 4 applies by virtue of
-- src:3541 | section 35(b) or who is a workman mentioned in section 35(a) —
-- src:3543 | (a) the employer must grant, and the employee must take, the
-- src:3544 | employee’s paid annual leave not later than 12 months after
-- src:3545 | the end of every 12 months of continuous service; and
@ref s 88A(6)(a) (src:3540-3545)
GIVEN `the answer of s 35(a)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the answer of s 35(b)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the day the 12 months of continuous service ended` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 88A(6)(a) the last day for taking the paid annual leave` MEANS
    IF `the answer of s 35(a)` EQUALS JUST TRUE OR `the answer of s 35(b)` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN JUST (`add months` `the day the 12 months of continuous service ended` 12)
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection is for a workman mentioned in section 35(a) or an employee to whom Part 4 applies by virtue of section 35(b), and the answer is not that; this type is a date and cannot say no, so the twin below does for a leave taken late (fork F18)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- The twin that can say no: the text fixes the period in its own words ("not later than 12 months after
-- the end of every 12 months of continuous service"), so leave taken after the last day is outside it
-- (ruling (a) of 2026-11-05).  The case of an employee outside s 35(a) and (b) is a scope limit ("In the
-- case of ...") and stays unanswered.
-- src:3540 | (6) In the case of an employee to whom Part 4 applies by virtue of
-- src:3541 | section 35(b) or who is a workman mentioned in section 35(a) —
-- src:3543 | (a) the employer must grant, and the employee must take, the
-- src:3544 | employee’s paid annual leave not later than 12 months after
-- src:3545 | the end of every 12 months of continuous service; and
@ref s 88A(6)(a) (src:3540-3545)
GIVEN `the answer of s 35(a)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the answer of s 35(b)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the day the 12 months of continuous service ended` IS A DATE
      `the day the paid annual leave is taken` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88A(6)(a) the paid annual leave is taken in time` MEANS
    IF `the answer of s 35(a)` EQUALS JUST TRUE OR `the answer of s 35(b)` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN
        IF `the day the paid annual leave is taken` AT MOST `add months` `the day the 12 months of continuous service ended` 12
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "not later than 12 months after the end of every 12 months of continuous service"
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection is "In the case of" a workman mentioned in section 35(a) or an employee to whom Part 4 applies by virtue of section 35(b)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3547 | (b) if the employee fails to take that leave by the end of that
-- src:3548 | period, the employee ceases to be entitled to that leave.
@ref s 88A(6)(b) (src:3547-3548)
GIVEN `the answer of s 35(a)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the answer of s 35(b)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the days of the paid annual leave not taken by the end of that period` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88A(6)(b) the employee is still entitled to the paid annual leave not taken` MEANS
    IF (`the answer of s 35(a)` EQUALS JUST TRUE OR `the answer of s 35(b)` EQUALS JUST TRUE)
       AND `the days of the paid annual leave not taken by the end of that period` GREATER THAN 0
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "the employee ceases to be entitled to that leave"
    THEN JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the paragraph never says she is still entitled, so the case where its "if" fails is answered by the twin `s 88A(6)(b) the employee ceases to be entitled to the paid annual leave not taken` below
    ELSE NOTHING

-- src:3547 | (b) if the employee fails to take that leave by the end of that
-- src:3548 | period, the employee ceases to be entitled to that leave.
@ref s 88A(6)(b) (src:3547-3548)
GIVEN `the answer of s 35(a)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the answer of s 35(b)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the days of the paid annual leave not taken by the end of that period` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 88A(6)(b) the employee ceases to be entitled to the paid annual leave not taken` MEANS
    IF `the answer of s 35(a)` EQUALS JUST TRUE OR `the answer of s 35(b)` EQUALS JUST TRUE
    THEN
        IF `the days of the paid annual leave not taken by the end of that period` GREATER THAN 0
        THEN JUST TRUE
        -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if the employee fails to take that leave by the end of that period"
        ELSE JUST FALSE
    -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the subsection is "In the case of" a workman mentioned in section 35(a) or an employee to whom Part 4 applies by virtue of section 35(b)
    ELSE NOTHING

-- s 88A(7): "An employer must pay an employee the employee's gross rate of pay for every day of paid
-- annual leave."  The gross rate for a day is the Third Schedule's answer (seam S6).
-- src:3552 | (7) An employer must pay an employee the employee’s gross rate
-- src:3553 | of pay for every day of paid annual leave.
@ref s 88A(7) (src:3552-3553)
GIVEN `the days of paid annual leave` IS A NUMBER
      `the answer of sch 3 gross rate of pay per day` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 88A(7) the amount the employer must pay for the paid annual leave` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the answer of sch 3 gross rate of pay per day`
    WHEN JUST rate THEN JUST (`the days of paid annual leave` TIMES rate)
    -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the gross rate of pay for a day is worked out under the Third Schedule, which is not given
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

-- s 88A(8): "If an employee is dismissed on any ground other than misconduct before the employee has
-- taken all of the employee's paid annual leave, the employer must pay the employee the employee's
-- gross rate of pay in respect of every day of that leave not taken by the employee."
-- src:3557 | (8) If an employee is dismissed on any ground other than
-- src:3558 | misconduct before the employee has taken all of the employee’s
-- src:3559 | paid annual leave, the employer must pay the employee the
-- src:3560 | employee’s gross rate of pay in respect of every day of that leave
-- src:3561 | not taken by the employee.
@ref s 88A(8) (src:3557-3561)
GIVEN `how the employment ended` IS A `How the employment ended`
      `the ground of dismissal` IS A `Ground of dismissal`
      `the days of paid annual leave not taken` IS A NUMBER
      `the answer of sch 3 gross rate of pay per day` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`s 88A(8) the amount the employer must pay for the paid annual leave not taken` MEANS
    IF `how the employment ended` EQUALS `Ending: the employer dismissed the employee`
       AND (NOT (`the ground of dismissal` EQUALS `Ground: misconduct inconsistent with the fulfilment of the conditions of service`))
       AND `the days of paid annual leave not taken` GREATER THAN 0
    THEN
        CONSIDER `the answer of sch 3 gross rate of pay per day`
        WHEN JUST rate THEN JUST (`the days of paid annual leave not taken` TIMES rate)
        -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the gross rate of pay for a day is worked out under the Third Schedule, which is not given
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
    -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If an employee is dismissed on any ground other than misconduct before the employee has"
    ELSE JUST 0
